<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hami</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kumul, Qumul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hami, or Kumul, is an oasis city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, at the eastern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> routes and the gateway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> it was ruled by Chagatayid princes, then by the Kumul Khanate under Qing protection until 1930. It is famous for its sweet Hami melons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hami.jpg" alt="Hami" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthew Summerton · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosque_in_Hami_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
